Title:
COM Hijacking AV/EDR Front-Ends to Reach SYSTEM via Back-End IPC (Part 1)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post analyzes a common AV/EDR architecture where a low-privileged UI “front-end” talks to a SYSTEM “back-end” service over IPC to perform privileged actions (settings changes, exclusions, updates). It shows how vendor trust checks (e.g., “request came from the signed UI”) can be undermined because malware can execute inside the trusted front-end process. The authors use COM hijacking (HKCU-over-HKLM CLSID resolution) to force the front-end to load an attacker-controlled in-proc COM DLL, proxying the original exports to avoid breaking functionality. They then inspect and replay named-pipe messages observed in plaintext to coerce the SYSTEM back-end into writing attacker-chosen registry values within a vendor-controlled subtree. By overwriting an “Application Path” registry value to point to a user-writable directory, they achieve SYSTEM execution after reboot when a privileged component loads binaries from that path. It’s primarily useful for red teamers and security researchers auditing EDR self-defense/IPC boundaries, and for blue teams to understand a recurring LPE pattern in security products.

Technical Focus:
- COM hijacking via HKCU\Software\Classes\CLSID precedence over HKLM
- In-proc COM DLL proxying (DllGetClassObject export forwarding)
- Front-end/back-end trust boundary failures in EDR architectures
- Named pipe traffic capture/analysis and message replay
- Privileged registry writes leading to SYSTEM LPE (path redirection)
- Tooling workflow: ProcMon/Sysinternals, IONinja Pipe Monitor, Donut shellcode

Use Cases:
- Identify hijackable COM CLSIDs used by a target UI process and gain code execution in-process
- Assess AV/EDR IPC channels (named pipes/RPC/COM) for replay/injection opportunities
- Develop LPE chains from “trusted UI” execution to SYSTEM back-end abuse
- Create detection ideas around suspicious HKCU CLSID overrides and unusual COM DLL loads
- Validate vendor self-defense claims by testing process hardening and IPC authorization

Keywords:
COM hijacking, CLSID, InProcServer32, HKCU, HKLM, CoCreateInstance, DllGetClassObject, DLL proxying, EDR self-defense, privilege escalation, SYSTEM, named pipes, CreateNamedPipe, ConnectNamedPipe, IPC replay, registry write, Application Path, ProcMon, Sysinternals, IONinja, Donut shellcode, Windows internals, LPE, 38c3